\name{BaseRAssocplotLayerProcessor}
\alias{BaseRAssocplotLayerProcessor}
\title{Base R Association Plot Layer Processor}
\description{
Processes Base R \code{assocplot()} layers -- a Cohen--Friendly association
plot, which draws one tile per cell of a two-way table whose signed height
is that cell's Pearson residual, \code{(observed - expected) / sqrt(expected)}.

Read as a \code{heat} layer, because a named grid of one number per cell is what
the chart states and row-then-column is how a reader navigates a
contingency table. Measured on \code{assocplot(HairEyeColor[, , 1])}, the rects
the drawing produces carry the residuals exactly:

\preformatted{
       Brown    Blue   Hazel   Green
Black  2.780  -2.059   0.184  -1.408
Brown  0.391  -0.246   0.185  -0.465
Red   -0.562  -0.658   0.532   1.485
Blond -3.273   3.271  -0.988   1.097
}

Nothing is inferred from the drawing: \code{assocplot()} is handed the table, so
the recorded call carries every number the trace wants. It returns \code{NULL},
which is why the reading comes from the argument -- the shape \code{bxp()}'s
reading took in #265.
\subsection{Two things this deliberately does not do}{

\strong{The tile width is dropped.} Each tile is drawn \code{sqrt(expected)} wide, so
the marginals are on the chart as a second encoding. A \code{heat} layer has no
width, and the residual is what an association plot exists to show -- the
eye reads height and sign, and the width is why a cell's box is wider
rather than a value the reader is asked to compare. Announcing it in \code{z}
instead would replace the number the chart is about.

\strong{It is not a \code{mosaic}.} \code{mosaicplot()} is read as one and the two look
alike, but a mosaic's tiles tile the space and carry proportions of a
whole. These float above and below a baseline and carry residuals, which
are signed and sum to nothing. Calling it a mosaic would tell a reader the
areas are shares of a total when they are a departure from an expectation.
}
