\subsection{\code{BaseRAssocplotLayerProcessor$extract_data()}}{
  Read the residual grid out of the recorded call.

The grid is the table \strong{transposed}, and its rows reversed. That is
not a convention chosen here but the relation \code{assocplot()} has to its
argument, measured from the drawn rects: the first dimension runs
across the x axis and the second up the y axis, bottom to top. It is
the same relation \code{image()} has to its matrix, and the heatmap
processor transposes for the same reason.

A table with a zero margin has cells whose expected count is zero, and
a residual there is \code{0/0}. \code{assocplot()} draws nothing for such a cell;
the grid has to keep a place for it, so it carries 0 -- the departure
from an expectation of nothing being nothing.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{BaseRAssocplotLayerProcessor$extract_data(layer_info)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{layer_info}}{Information about the recorded plot call}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    List with points, x and y, empty when there is no table
  }
}

